Ariadne Tree installed-library tutorials

The C++ executable tutorial_tree.cpp demonstrates both BinaryTreeNode and
DerivationTree. It uses the installed public header ariadne-tree.hpp, just as a
separate application would. No Evolution dependency is needed.

After installing Tree to a prefix:

  cmake -S tutorial -B tutorial-build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/path/to/install
  cmake --build tutorial-build
  ./tutorial-build/tutorial_tree

On a multi-configuration generator, select a configuration while building and
run the executable in that configuration's directory, for example:

  cmake --build tutorial-build --config Debug
  ./tutorial-build/Debug/tutorial_tree

C++ walkthrough: an object and its metadata
-----------------------------------------

The DerivationTree example deliberately uses two small application types:

  struct SampleSeries {
      std::vector<int> values;
  };

  struct SampleMetadata {
      std::string label;
      bool complete;
  };

  using SampleTree = Ariadne::DerivationTree<SampleSeries, SampleMetadata>;

The object contains an ordered sequence of samples. Metadata gives the node a
readable label and a local completion flag. Tree stores these types without
interpreting the samples or deciding when work is complete.

1. Create the root

   create_root starts the source node with samples [1, 2]. root() returns an
   optional ID: an empty tree has no root. Store the returned opaque NodeId;
   do not infer a position or ordering from its diagnostic representation.

2. Derive two children

   add_child creates doubled with [4] and tripled with [6], both derived from
   the last source sample, 2. The new objects are explicitly supplied by the
   example: the generic tree does not calculate or copy them automatically.
   children(source) retains the order doubled, tripled.

3. Continue an existing object

   object(source).values.push_back(3) changes the existing source object.
   Appending 6 to doubled changes that child's object. Neither update creates
   a node. Setting metadata(source).complete = true completes only source;
   the children still need work.

4. Inspect genealogy

   doubled creates offset with [5]. parent(offset) is doubled;
   ancestors(offset) returns doubled, source. descendants(source) uses
   depth-first preorder and excludes source itself. The printing helpers use
   const object/metadata access to show this genealogy:

     source [1, 2, 3] complete
       doubled [4, 6] complete
         offset [5] needs work
       tripled [6] needs work

5. Prune at a retained checkpoint

   prune(doubled) returns 1 and removes offset. doubled stays in the same
   position with the same ID, samples [4, 6] and complete flag. source and
   tripled are unchanged. contains(offset) becomes false; object(offset)
   throws std::out_of_range. Discard views/references to removed nodes and
   reacquire the pruning boundary's children view after pruning.

6. Regrow using add_child

   The application explicitly resets doubled's completion flag and appends 8.
   add_child then creates replacement with [9] under doubled:

     source [1, 2, 3] complete
       doubled [4, 6, 8] needs work
         replacement [9] needs work
       tripled [6] needs work

   replacement has a different ID from offset. The old ID remains invalid
   even if the implementation reuses its storage slot. Retained payload
   references survive growth and pruning elsewhere; their internal vector
   iterators still follow the usual std::vector lifetime rules.

The program prints the before/pruned/regrown states, checks the relationships
and expected values, and finishes with "Ariadne Tree tutorial passed." It exits
with a nonzero status and a diagnostic if a DerivationTree expectation fails.
The C++ tutorial executable is already built and run by the platform CI jobs.

Python example
--------------

When the Python extension has been installed:

  python tutorial/tutorial_tree.py

The Python example demonstrates the existing BinaryTreeNode binding. The
custom Object/Metadata DerivationTree example above is a C++ template example.
